# The 14 / 0, with the exact command that produced it (T4, round 2).
#
# WHY THE COMMAND IS HERE. The first version of this file held only the numbers, and the live
# script's step 1 -- which is supposed to be where they are proved -- did not produce them on the
# run recorded in `reimport.log`: its `grep -c` was mis-quoted and printed `…20260903063722_world.sql:0`
# for both counts. The numbers below were always read by hand, from this command, against the same
# image; the script was repaired afterwards and its step 1 has NOT been re-run end to end. So the
# command belongs beside the output, and this file is the proof rather than the script's log.
#
# Read-only: one throwaway container against the image on the tag compose uses. Nothing on the
# install is touched and nothing is started; the stack stayed stopped throughout.
#
# Run 2026-09-09 (round 2), from the laptop:
#
# ssh m910q "sudo docker run --rm --entrypoint sh yulon.local/cmangos-tortoise-server:native-58c6fd1c \
#   -c 'F=/opt/tortoise/sql/database_updates/world/20260903063722_world.sql; \
#       echo \"INSERT IGNORE INTO: \$(grep -c INSERT.IGNORE.INTO \$F)\"; \
#       echo \"bare INSERT INTO: \$(grep -c ^INSERT.INTO \$F)\"; \
#       echo \"sha1 of the rewritten file: \$(sha1sum \$F | cut -c1-40)\"; \
#       grep -m1 PRETTY /etc/os-release'"
#
# `INSERT.IGNORE.INTO` and `^INSERT.INTO` are the same two patterns the ticket names, spelled with
# `.` for the spaces so no quoting survives a trip through ssh, a remote shell and `sh -c` -- the
# exact failure the script's own step 1 met. `^INSERT.INTO` cannot match `INSERT IGNORE INTO`.
#
INSERT IGNORE INTO: 14
bare INSERT INTO: 0
sha1 of the rewritten file: 7a3a97e96c19e5b1188da8dbe1018c9b4be61791
PRETTY_NAME="Ubuntu 24.04.4 LTS"
